  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Maquon’s name comes from the Potawatomi word “Amaquonsippi,” literally meaning “mussel river.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maquon%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s historic schoolhouse, a simple white clapboard building, has watched generations of Maquon residents grow up.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/relaxed-town-illinois/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1837, settlers Pernach Owen and Elisha Thurman platted the village as Bennington before renaming it Maquon in 1858.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maquon%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Maquon sits on a low bluff just north and west of the Spoon River, giving it a gentle rise above the water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maquon%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Archaeologists have found pottery shards near Maquon dated to 350 BC, showing the area was inhabited long before settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.maquon.org/about-us-1"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In Maquon, rush hour often means waiting for a tractor to make its way down Main Street, a charming rural rhythm.",
